= 1
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For one oscillator, set $x=\hbar\Omega/(k_BT)$. Its <quantum-oscillator energy and heat capacity> are
$$
\boxed{U=\hbar\Omega\left(\frac12+\frac1{e^x-1}\right),\qquad
C_V=k_B\frac{x^2e^x}{(e^x-1)^2}.}
$$
At low <temperature>, $U\to\hbar\Omega/2$ and $C_V\sim k_Bx^2e^{-x}\to0$: excitations freeze out but <zero-point energy> remains. At high <temperature>, $U\sim k_BT$ and $C_V\to k_B$, recovering <equipartition>. The zero-point term does not contribute to <heat capacity>.

\Image[paper-1-tsp2014-oscillator.png]
{title=Original quantum-oscillator energy and heat-capacity curves, with zero-point energy, low-temperature freeze-out and classical high-temperature limits}

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Using $C_V=T(\partial S/\partial T)_V$ and the stated <Maxwell relation>, interchange derivatives within a smooth single <phase>:
$$
\left(\frac{\partial C_V}{\partial V}\right)_T
=T\frac{\partial}{\partial T}\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_T
=T\left(\frac{\partial^2p}{\partial T^2}\right)_V=0.
$$
Therefore **$C_V$ depends only on <temperature>** for the given temperature-linear <equation of state>. It need not be constant in <temperature>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $N$ identical classical noninteracting particles, use $Z_N=Z_1^N/N!$. At fixed $N$,
$$
\ln Z_N=N\ln V+\frac{3N}{2}\ln T+\text{constant},
$$
so the canonical partition-function identities give
$$
p=k_BT\left(\frac{\partial\ln Z_N}{\partial V}\right)_T=\frac{Nk_BT}{V},\qquad
U=k_BT^2\left(\frac{\partial\ln Z_N}{\partial T}\right)_V=\frac32Nk_BT.
$$
Thus, for one <mole>,
$$
\boxed{pV=RT,\qquad C_{V,m}=\frac32R.}
$$
The supplied $T^{3/2}$ dependence includes only three translational degrees of freedom; additional thermally active internal <modes> would change the <heat capacity>.

= 2
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For one Cartesian <velocity> component of a free Brownian particle, the inertial <Langevin equation> is
$$
m\dot v=-\zeta v+\xi(t),\qquad
\langle\xi(t)\rangle=0,\qquad
\langle\xi(t)\xi(t')\rangle=2\zeta k_BT\delta(t-t').
$$
The random <force> and viscous drag obey the fluctuation-dissipation relation. With a <thermal equilibrium> <velocity> distribution, the <relaxation time> is $\tau=m/\zeta$ and
$$
\langle v(t)v(0)\rangle=\frac{k_BT}{m}e^{-|t|/\tau}.
$$
Integrating <velocity> twice gives
$$
\boxed{\langle[x(t)-x(0)]^2\rangle
=2\frac{k_BT}{\zeta}\left[t-\tau(1-e^{-t/\tau})\right].}
$$
For $t\gg\tau$, this is Einstein's <diffusion> law, $\langle\Delta x^2\rangle=2Dt$, with **$D=k_BT/\zeta$**. In three dimensions $\langle|\Delta\mathbf r|^2\rangle=6Dt$. At $t\ll\tau$ the motion is ballistic, $\langle\Delta x^2\rangle\simeq(k_BT/m)t^2$. A sphere in the Stokes-drag regime has $\zeta=6\pi\eta a$, giving $D=k_BT/(6\pi\eta a)$.

\Image[paper-1-tsp2014-brownian.png]
{title=Original equilibrium Langevin mean-square displacement, showing the ballistic-to-diffusive crossover and the factor-of-two one-coordinate diffusion convention}

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Landau theory> expands the <free energy> in an <order parameter>. For a scalar with sign-reversal symmetry and zero <conjugate field>, a useful <sextic even Landau potential> is
$$
f(m)=f_0+\frac r2m^2+\frac b4m^4+\frac c6m^6,
\qquad r=a(T-T_0),\quad a,c>0.
$$
Equilibrium selects the <global minimum>; other local minima can be metastable.

For $b>0$, the minimum changes continuously from $m=0$ to $m\simeq\pm\sqrt{-r/b}$ as $r$ passes below zero. This is a second-order transition at $T_0$: the <order parameter> is continuous, there is no <latent heat>, and thermodynamic derivatives such as <heat capacity> can be discontinuous.

For $b<0$, the sixth-order term stabilizes the <free energy>. Equality of the ordered and disordered minima, together with $f'(m)=0$, gives
$$
\boxed{m_t^2=-\frac{3b}{4c},\qquad r_t=\frac{3b^2}{16c}>0.}
$$
The <order parameter> jumps at $T_t=T_0+r_t/a$. The <entropy> generally jumps too, giving <latent heat>; metastable branches and barriers permit <hysteresis>, though equilibrium coexistence itself does not require <hysteresis>. The special case $b=0$ is tricritical. Mean-field exponents can fail where fluctuations dominate.

\Image[paper-1-tsp2014-landau.png]
{title=Original even Landau free-energy curves: continuous quartic ordering and sextic first-order coexistence with separated equal-depth minima}

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At $T\ll T_F$, Pauli exclusion restricts thermally changed occupations to an <energy> interval of order $k_BT$ about the <Fermi energy>. The affected fraction is of order $T/T_F$; their typical excitation <energy> is of order $k_BT$, so the thermal <energy> correction is proportional to $T^2$ and the <heat capacity> is linear in $T$.

More precisely, with $g(E_F)$ the total <density> of one-particle states per unit <energy>, including <spin>,
$$
\boxed{C_V=\frac{\pi^2}{3}g(E_F)k_B^2T+O(T^3).}
$$
For a three-dimensional <ideal gas> with quadratic dispersion, $g(E_F)=3N/(2E_F)$, hence
$$
\boxed{C_V=\frac{\pi^2}{2}Nk_B\frac{T}{T_F},\qquad T_F=E_F/k_B.}
$$
The fixed-particle-number <Sommerfeld expansion> gives $U=3NE_F/5+(\pi^2/4)N(k_BT)^2/E_F+\cdots$. The <temperature> dependence of the <chemical potential> must be included when obtaining this expression; holding it artificially fixed changes the intermediate <energy> correction.

= 3
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For a single quantum state, the equilibrium occupations are
$$
\boxed{\bar n_F(\epsilon)=\frac1{e^{(\epsilon-\mu)/(k_BT)}+1},\qquad
\bar n_B(\epsilon)=\frac1{e^{(\epsilon-\mu)/(k_BT)}-1}.}
$$
Both reduce to $e^{(\mu-\epsilon)/(k_BT)}$ when $\epsilon-\mu\gg k_BT$.

For one three-dimensional quadratic branch, the <density of states> per unit <volume> is
$$
g(\epsilon)=\frac1{4\pi^2}\left(\frac{2m}{\hbar^2}\right)^{3/2}\sqrt\epsilon.
$$
With $\lambda_T=\sqrt{2\pi\hbar^2/(mk_BT)}$, changing integration variable to $x=\epsilon/(k_BT)$ gives
$$
\boxed{n_{\rm ex}(\mu=0)=\frac{\zeta(3/2)}{\lambda_T^3}\propto T^{3/2},\qquad
n_{\rm classical}=\frac{e^{\mu/(k_BT)}}{\lambda_T^3}.}
$$
A degeneracy factor multiplies both <densities> if additional equivalent branches are present. The first expression is the noncondensed <density>: a macroscopically occupied <ground state> must be counted separately.

<Bose-Einstein condensation> occurs when the <excited states> can no longer hold all particles and the ground-state occupation becomes macroscopic. At fixed total <density>,
$$
\boxed{T_c=\frac{2\pi\hbar^2}{mk_B}
\left[\frac{n}{\zeta(3/2)}\right]^{2/3}\propto n^{2/3}.}
$$
For <field-induced triplon condensation>, write $\Delta=\nu n-\eta(H-H_g)$, so $\epsilon(k)=\hbar^2k^2/(2m)+\Delta$. At fixed reference $n$, the three requested dispersions have gaps $\nu n+\eta H_g$, $\nu n$ and zero respectively. Their curvature is unchanged. Because the actual thermodynamic <chemical potential> is zero, the Bose distribution requires $\Delta\ge0$. At $H_c=H_g+\nu n/\eta$, the minimum reaches zero and the noncondensed population saturates, permitting macroscopic occupation.

For the <triplon condensation field scaling>, the critical <density> at onset is $n_T=\zeta(3/2)/\lambda_T^3$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{H_c(T)-H_g=\frac{\nu}{\eta}\zeta(3/2)
\left(\frac{mk_BT}{2\pi\hbar^2}\right)^{3/2}\propto T^{3/2}.}
$$
The low-temperature <phase> boundary rises from $(T,H)=(0,H_g)$; the condensed <phase> lies on the higher-field side.

\Image[paper-1-tsp2014-triplons.png]
{title=Original shifted-parabola triplon dispersions and low-temperature field–temperature phase diagram; model choices are dimensionless and illustrative}

For $H>H_c$, simply continuing the gap below zero would give unphysical Bose occupations. For the <condensate density in a shifted-parabola triplon model>, added <condensate density> keeps the minimum at zero: $\nu(n_0+n_T)=\eta(H-H_g)$. At fixed <temperature> this gives
$$
\boxed{n_0=\frac{\eta}{\nu}[H-H_c(T)]\quad(H>H_c),}
$$
with $n_0=0$ below onset. At zero <temperature> it becomes $n_0=\eta(H-H_g)/\nu$ within that model.

This prefactor is conditional on continuing the stated single-density mean-field shift into the condensed <phase>. A microscopic interacting Bose condensate has collective Bogoliubov excitations $E_k=\sqrt{\epsilon_k^0(\epsilon_k^0+2g n_0)}$, with $\epsilon_k^0=\hbar^2k^2/(2m)$, which are linear at small $k$. The normal-state shift alone does not fix the condensed interaction closure or identify $g$ with $\nu$. **The gap closes and the <condensate density> grows linearly with excess field in the simple mean-field description; the detailed collective dispersion needs that additional interaction model.** A dilute-boson treatment of field-induced magnetic ordering makes this normal/condensed distinction explicitly.<Bose-Einstein Condensation of Dilute Magnons in TlCuCl3 by Tetsuro Nikuni and others>[arXiv:cond-mat/9908118v1, equations (4)–(9): normal Hartree-Fock shift, T^(3/2) onset and gapless condensed spectrum]{ref}

= 4
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Use $v$ for molar <volume> to distinguish it from the fluctuating parcel <volume> below. The equation is $p=RT/(v-b)-a/v^2$. The parameter $b>0$ models excluded <volume>, while $a>0$ models the reduction in pressure from attractive interactions.

Below the <critical temperature>, a raw isotherm has a loop. The positive-slope segment is mechanically unstable and surrounding metastable portions do not give the stable equilibrium mixture. Replace the loop by a constant coexistence pressure $p_s$ satisfying the Maxwell equal-area condition,
$$
\int_{v_l}^{v_g}[p(v,T)-p_s],dv=0.
$$
The endpoints are liquid and vapour molar <volumes>; intermediate mean <volumes> correspond to <phase> mixtures with changing proportions. Above the <critical temperature>, liquid-like and gas-like states connect continuously without a coexistence plateau.

\Image[paper-1-tsp2014-van-der-waals.png]
{title=Original reduced van der Waals isotherms with computed Maxwell coexistence plateaus and the pressure–temperature coexistence curve ending at the critical point}

In the pressure-temperature diagram, the liquid-vapour coexistence line ends at the <critical point>. Below it, the low-pressure side is vapour and the high-pressure side liquid. “Gas” conventionally denotes the region above the <critical temperature>, where compression alone cannot cross a liquid-vapour transition. The model has no solid <phase>.

The critical isotherm has an inflection: $p_v=p_{vv}=0$. Thus
$$
-\frac{RT_c}{(v_c-b)^2}+\frac{2a}{v_c^3}=0,\qquad
\frac{2RT_c}{(v_c-b)^3}-\frac{6a}{v_c^4}=0,
$$
which give
$$
\boxed{v_c=3b,\qquad T_c=\frac{8a}{27Rb},\qquad p_c=\frac{a}{27b^2}.}
$$
For fluctuations about a stable equilibrium value $x_0$, the system plus reservoir has probability proportional to $e^{-A(x)/(k_BT)}$. Expanding <availability>,
$$
A(x)=A(x_0)+\frac12 A''(x_0)(x-x_0)^2+\cdots,
$$
gives a <Gaussian distribution> and, to this order, $\langle x\rangle=x_0$ with
$$
\boxed{\langle(x-\langle x\rangle)^2\rangle\simeq\frac{k_BT}{A''(x_0)}.}
$$
This requires positive curvature and sufficiently small fluctuations; it is not exact when higher-order terms matter.

The whole cylinder's <volume> is fixed. For the <volume fluctuations of a small fluid parcel>, interpret the small quantity as a material parcel containing $s$ <moles>, whose <volume> can fluctuate against the much larger remainder. Its mean <volume> at the critical <density> is $\bar V=s v_c=3sb$. At fixed <temperature> its <availability> is $A(V)=F_s(V,T)+p_{\rm bath}V$, so
$$
A_{VV}=-\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial V}\right)_{T,s}
=-\frac1s\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial v}\right)_T.
$$
On the critical isochore, approached from $T>T_c$,
$$
\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial v}\right)_{v_c,T}
=-\frac{R(T-T_c)}{4b^2}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\langle\delta V^2\rangle
\simeq\frac{4k_Bs b^2T}{R(T-T_c)}
=\frac{4k_B b\bar V T}{3R(T-T_c)}.}
$$
Equivalently, $\langle\delta V^2\rangle=k_BT\kappa_T\bar V$, with $\kappa_T=4b/[3R(T-T_c)]$. **The mean-field <variance> diverges as $(T-T_c)^{-1}$, so its <critical exponent> is $1$**; the root-mean-square fluctuation has exponent $1/2$. A fixed spatial observation box instead has number fluctuations, not fluctuating geometric <volume>. Finite size and non-Gaussian terms cut off the literal divergence; the approximation fails exactly at the <critical point>.
